WEBVTT

1
00:00:11.200 --> 00:00:17.743
When Laurie got home from school, she ran straight to the mailbox.

2
00:00:17.744 --> 00:00:25.101
Grandpa had gone to the Southwest a week ago but hadn't sent her anything yet.

3
00:00:25.102 --> 00:00:34.409
Had he forgotten about her? She saw a postcard and a yellow envelope addressed to her. "Finally!" she thought.

4
00:00:34.410 --> 00:00:41.431
Hi, Laurie! How's my favorite granddaughter? I made it to New Mexico.

5
00:00:41.432 --> 00:00:53.789
I'm in Chaco Culture National Historical Park studying the ancient Pueblos—Native Americans who lived in the Southwest over 1,500 years ago.

6
00:00:53.790 --> 00:01:01.670
Many different peoples lived here, but as a group, we call them the Pueblos, after the houses they built.

7
00:01:01.671 --> 00:01:07.402
Their houses had connecting walls and rooms built on top of one another.

8
00:01:07.403 --> 00:01:12.367
They were really more like villages than individual houses.

9
00:01:12.368 --> 00:01:17.444
The biggest Pueblo settlements are found here in Chaco Canyon.

10
00:01:17.445 --> 00:01:21.810
We archaeologists call them the great houses.

11
00:01:21.811 --> 00:01:30.968
One house, Pueblo Bonito, covered about three acres, and probably housed about twelve hundred people.

12
00:01:30.969 --> 00:01:37.969
Pueblo society had no leaders, so living together took a lot of organization and patience.

13
00:01:37.970 --> 00:01:44.455
But amazingly, they were one of the most peaceful people this country has ever known.

14
00:01:44.456 --> 00:01:49.430
I'll write you a letter explaining more when I set up camp tonight.

15
00:01:49.431 --> 00:01:54.598
But I had to send you a postcard of the amazing Pueblo Bonito!

16
00:01:54.599 --> 00:01:58.028
Laurie examined the image on the postcard.

17
00:01:58.029 --> 00:02:06.320
All the walls really were connected! She tried to imagine living in a house that was connected to everybody else's.

18
00:02:06.321 --> 00:02:11.438
It would be strange to live so close to her classmates and teacher!

19
00:02:11.439 --> 00:02:15.845
She wished she could have gone with Grandpa to see the ancient sites.

20
00:02:15.846 --> 00:02:19.320
There were so many things she wanted to know.

21
00:02:19.321 --> 00:02:25.820
If the Pueblos moved into the Southwest 1,500 years ago, where were they before that?

22
00:02:25.821 --> 00:02:32.761
Where were they now? She opened the envelope and started reading Grandpa's letter.

23
00:02:32.762 --> 00:02:33.979
Dear Laurie,

24
00:02:33.980 --> 00:02:37.872
I know you wish you were here and I do too.

25
00:02:37.873 --> 00:02:45.714
I bet you also have lots of questions about the Pueblos. So let's start at the beginning.

26
00:02:45.715 --> 00:02:54.678
Native Americans had been living in the United States for thousands of years before the Europeans arrived and colonized the land.

27
00:02:54.679 --> 00:03:02.951
Many historians think the natives originally came from Asia and arrived in North America during the Ice Age.

28
00:03:02.952 --> 00:03:07.894
Back then a land bridge connected Asia to Alaska.

29
00:03:07.895 --> 00:03:16.365
Native Americans might have crossed this land bridge while following bison and other large mammals they hunted.

30
00:03:16.366 --> 00:03:20.687
But Pueblos have their own version of how they got here.

31
00:03:20.688 --> 00:03:29.112
They believe they came from the earth, in a holy place underground, and were then led to the surface by a god.

32
00:03:29.113 --> 00:03:36.663
Origins aside, we know for sure that the first Pueblos were nomadic hunters and gatherers.

33
00:03:36.664 --> 00:03:48.497
Eventually they began building pit houses. These were circular underground houses, dug five or six feet into the ground.

34
00:03:48.498 --> 00:03:57.738
Around the year 700, they began building aboveground, but kept underground rooms for special religious ceremonies.

35
00:03:57.739 --> 00:04:04.862
Now their houses were made of stone and red-clay bricks, but still had no doors or windows.

36
00:04:04.863 --> 00:04:12.703
So how did the Pueblos get in and out of their houses? Through the roof!

37
00:04:12.704 --> 00:04:19.698
They climbed up a wooden ladder to the top of the wall, and then down another ladder to enter their homes.

38
00:04:19.699 --> 00:04:26.145
Imagine you're returning home after a hunt, carrying a large deer over your back.

39
00:04:26.146 --> 00:04:31.613
Or you're coming back from the spring with two large jugs full of water!

40
00:04:31.614 --> 00:04:36.337
You would become a very skilled ladder climber!

41
00:04:36.338 --> 00:04:41.749
Maybe you'd like to know a little more about the Pueblos' daily life.

42
00:04:41.750 --> 00:04:49.731
They worked very hard. Women ground corn and gathered seeds, berries, and nuts for the family meals.

43
00:04:49.732 --> 00:04:57.501
They also made pottery. Men took care of the crops, went on hunts, and wove baskets and blankets.

44
00:04:57.502 --> 00:05:01.351
Young children and girls stayed home to help their mothers.

45
00:05:01.352 --> 00:05:08.304
But older boys followed the men, learning everything they would need to know to become adults in the village.

46
00:05:08.305 --> 00:05:15.004
It was hot during the daytime, when the plateaus were lit by the sun, but nights were cold.

47
00:05:15.005 --> 00:05:21.838
The Pueblos ate warm cornbread, beans, and squash, and roasted meat over fires.

48
00:05:21.839 --> 00:05:31.103
Then they curled up in rabbit-skin robes and turkey-feather blankets and went to sleep because at sunrise they would start another long day.

49
00:05:31.104 --> 00:05:37.449
But guess what these hardworking people did in their free time, Laurie?

50
00:05:37.450 --> 00:05:45.616
They played games! Running races, dart-throwing contests, and ball games were all popular.

51
00:05:45.617 --> 00:05:53.316
Of course the games were fun, but Pueblos also believed that these games were invented by the gods.

52
00:05:53.317 --> 00:06:01.623
So playing them pleased the gods and helped keep rivers flowing, rain falling, and the sun shining in the sky.

53
00:06:01.624 --> 00:06:10.788
When I get back, let's see if playing kickball every day will keep the gods happy and bring us lots of good sledding snow this winter!

54
00:06:10.789 --> 00:06:12.953
Missing you, Grandpa

55
00:06:12.954 --> 00:06:23.448
P.S. I'll send you an email from the next site.

56
00:06:23.449 --> 00:06:32.638
Laurie ran upstairs to the computer in the study.

57
00:06:32.639 --> 00:06:37.508
In her inbox was an e-card from Grandpa.

58
00:06:37.509 --> 00:06:42.271
Laurie—I'm in Mesa Verde National Park in Colorado.

59
00:06:42.272 --> 00:06:49.273
Here Pueblos farmed, built houses, wove baskets, and performed rain dances.

60
00:06:49.274 --> 00:06:58.821
But in the 1200s, they moved down into the cliffs and built their homes into the steep walls of canyons, perhaps for protection.

61
00:06:58.822 --> 00:07:05.389
These peaceful people spent their time making pottery and offering respect to the gods.

62
00:07:05.390 --> 00:07:12.065
Then, about a hundred years later, they mysteriously left Mesa Verde forever.

63
00:07:12.066 --> 00:07:18.715
Were they escaping something or had they simply outgrown their homes? No one knows.

64
00:07:18.716 --> 00:07:28.212
They continued to live nearby until the Spanish arrived in the 1500s and forced the Pueblos to leave their homes yet again.

65
00:07:28.213 --> 00:07:32.626
But Pueblo ancestors still live in the Southwest.

66
00:07:32.627 --> 00:07:41.235
In fact, I'll meet some modern Pueblos tomorrow at a ceremony! See you soon! –Grandpa

67
00:07:41.236 --> 00:07:44.705
Laurie looked at the photograph on the e-card.

68
00:07:44.706 --> 00:07:51.538
She couldn't imagine climbing in and out of that big canyon every day just to get home.

69
00:07:51.539 --> 00:07:55.527
The Pueblos must have been so strong!

70
00:07:55.528 --> 00:08:03.707
Laurie heard the front door open. "Laurie! Are you home? Sorry I'm late!" Mom called.

71
00:08:03.708 --> 00:08:13.651
"I'm up here, Mom! I got mail from Grandpa in New Mexico!"
